e":"","content":"Nikink, where you the person on the previous forum who was doing First Tongue translations? By the way, this is amazing!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/444-dessembrae","name":"Dessembrae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar444_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"362"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T02:44:48-07:00","id":105465,"title":"","content":"Nope, that wasn't me. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The key finding of this project is that the 'step 3' of the process as stated by Ethan is the most important. Many first tongue examples are different in ways unexplainable by Grimm's Law, some examples are where the author applied Grimm's Law forwards rather than backwards, and some words are simply no relation at all - Auspices for example, can at best be called 'inspired by'. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I hope to get enough guidelines nutted out to account for most changes between the Sumerian and the final word, and this to be able to create a much better fitting transformation. But it's tough to find the original sumerian that inspired certain First Tongue words.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/ofii86boiyk38ng/DtDRandomForms.zip\" target=\"_blank\">Open the gates of Hell: Random Demon Generator.</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1X6qQzoAPrSRNzl3cQcMWasoPv3eK56sIlAOTeay61No/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Gather an army of Rage! Random Garou Generator.</a><br /